      "text": "To determine the value of \\( n \\) that makes the seven-digit number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by \\( 11 \\), we can use the rule for divisibility by \\( 11 \\). The rule states that a number is divisible by \\( 11 \\) if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of \\( 11 \\).\n\nIn the number \\( 854n526 \\), the digits in the odd positions are \\( 8, 4, 5, \\) and \\( 6 \\), and the digits in the even positions are \\( 5, n, \\) and \\( 2 \\). Let's denote the sum of the digits in the odd positions as \\( S_o \\) and the sum of the digits in the even positions as \\( S_e \\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\( S_o \\):\n\\[\nS_o = 8 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 23\n\\]\nNext, we calculate \\( S_e \\):\n\\[\nS_e = 5 + n + 2 = n + 7\n\\]\nThe difference \\( S_o - S_e \\) must be a multiple of \\( 11 \\):\n\\[\n23 - (n + 7) = 16 - n\n\\]\nSo, \\( 16 - n \\) must be a multiple of \\( 11 \\). The possible values for \\( 16 - n \\) that are multiples of \\( 11 \\) are \\( -11, 0, 11 \\). Since \\( n \\) is a digit (i.e., \\( n \\) is an integer between \\( 0 \\) and \\( 9 \\)), the only possible value is \\( n = 16 \\). However, \\( n \\) must also be a digit, so \\( n = 16 \\) is not a valid choice. Instead, we consider the positive multiples that make \\( n \\) a valid digit:\n\\[\n16 - n = -11 \\implies n = 27 \\quad \\text{(not a valid digit)}\n\\]\n\\[\n16 - n = 0 \\implies n = 16 \\quad \\text{(not a valid digit)}\n\\]\n\\[\n16 - n = 11 \\implies n = 5 \\quad \\text{(a valid digit)}\n\\]\nThus, the only valid digit for \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is \\( n = 5 \\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
